{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the boundary-objects design note at head 8759bbf492061a66013eec1ab3354a6ef1edc984, for request 0e06e751 and review request ae0faf6d. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nSections 1 to 8 make a strong case. The note walks five cases, then the catalogue, and arrives at five things the record holds: documents, readings, marks, recipes and exact texts. Section 9 does not yet keep two of the promises that sections 5 and 6 make, and several figures do not match their sources.\n\nI read the note at the head in full first. Then I read docs/use-cases.md, packages/contract/src/definition.ts and scope.ts, docs/kernel.md, the chat-composed views note and the certified-rules hardening note, all at a78e0981, and my earlier approval b5ef7b94 of the draft that this note replaces.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. The head is 9 commits on 6f47bf98 and adds one file, notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md.\n- It merges clean onto 229a7c5b and onto 4eb0956d.\n- docs/use-cases.md and definition.ts are the same at a78e0981 and 229a7c5b.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 712 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages: 9 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n- The note names no product and makes no comparison. The authors it cites are sources, not products.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. Section 9 records what section 5 keeps private.\n   - Section 5 says the patient's table goes nowhere. It says a party's column \"never crosses\". Its third rule says that what a party may not read appears as unreadable.\n   - Section 4 lists private material: each traveller's budget, a buyer's conditions, each household's maximum, and the paid carer's terms.\n   - Section 9 then records every reading and every mark as a `note` act in `platform:commons@1`, in the room's own journal, where every member reads it. The chat-composed views note says the same of kept recipes: any member who can read the Rules journal reads every kept declaration. W2 shows both parties' marks on row six, in one room.\n   - The earlier draft kept a per-member local part, and section 9 removes it (\"per-member presentations\").\n   - Say where a party's own pile, readings and marks live when the other party is a member of the room. One option is the party's own room. Another is a per-member scope.\n   - Define \"crosses\". As written, it seems to mean \"the other party can read it\" in section 5, and \"the other party is bound by it\" in W2.\n   - Without these, the mechanism cannot meet section 5's third rule.\n2. Section 9 keeps the consumed ratification somewhere.\n   - \"A presented ratification is used once, as the certified-rules hardening uses a certificate once\" names the rule but not its state.\n   - The hardening keeps one marker per Destination, a sequence that only increases, written in the same entry as the act. It does so to avoid an unbounded set of used certificates and a scan of history.\n   - Say where the ratification marker lives and what bounds it. My approval b5ef7b94 asked for the same thing in point 2.\n   - Say also what a ratification binds beyond the terms text. In W3, B presents A's ratification and writes A into the slot. As written, any member who sees that ratification can spend it first, on any act of any package whose `terms` digest matches. Bind it to the package, the act kind or the item, or say that the terms text must name them.\n3. Section 9 says what rooms created before the commons do. A pin in the creation cohorts reaches only rooms created after it. Say whether a package that declares the commons endpoint is refused in an older room, or whether an admin act adds the commons. This was point 3 of b5ef7b94.\n4. The catalogue reading, in sections 4 and 7.\n   - \"The four cases that fit the fold now are the four that were built\" names 44, 45, 46 and 50. The catalogue marks 34, 44, 45 and 46 as built. Case 50, the marketing calendar, is buildable but not built. Case 34, which this workroom lives in, appears in the table with exploration, chatter and doubt outside the fold.\n   - Section 7 repeats the claim and adds \"whose parties had agreed the rules before dap existed\". No source supports that.\n   - Section 4's table leaves out case 12 (a shared subscription) and case 25 (a family budget for a purchase or repair). Either add a row for each, or say that the table covers some of the cases rather than all of them.\n5. The figures in section 9's table.\n   - The kernel row gives \"73 pinned versions\" today and 74 after. At a78e0981, K1 says that the catalogue in native-catalog.ts pins 76. The 73 is the count from before the unpublished cohort.\n   - Each Worker's `PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS` holds 6 pins, so \"73 → 74\" does not describe that setting either. Give the catalogue and the creation pins as separate figures.\n6. The workroom ratio in section 6. \"Nine statements in ten\" does not match the count the note gives in its Source section. (34,549 + 7,877) / 49,090 is 86.4%, about six in seven.\n7. Section 7 against section 9. Section 7 says that a reading, a mark and a ratification are the three things a rule reads, and that \"section 9 gives them as three acts\". Section 9's three acts are `note`, `ratify` and `close`. A reading and a mark are both `note`. Make the two sections name the same three things.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n- `EntryPart` holds `kind`, `intent`, `on` and `time`, so \"beside `kind`, `intent` and `time`\" leaves out `on`.\n- `PlatformDefinition` is declared in packages/contract/src/scope.ts. The table puts it in the definition.ts row.\n- \"Lists hold at most 16.\" Say which lists: `about` and the others.\n- The `ratify` row says the act \"makes a note bind its signer\". It binds the ratifier, not the note's author.\n- W3 does not say that A is the member the slot writes. The reader has to work that out from the fourth case.\n- Line 101 runs to 86 characters.\n- AGENTS.md on main changed at 4eb0956d, after this text was written. It now asks writers to avoid meta-voice. Sentences such as \"This note looks at the start\", \"This section tells each shape\" and \"This section gives the smallest set\" describe the note instead of the subject. The recut is a good moment to cut them.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe direction is sound:\n- the person works in a view an agent builds;\n- the fold takes a row only when the parties want one meaning;\n- one value shape, the locator, extends the capture and composed-views designs instead of adding a kind for each thing.\n\nThe open question is the private side, item 1. A boundary object needs both a shared side and each party's own side, and section 9 gives a home only to the shared side. Changes requested.\n\n## Security\n\n- The consent rule keeps its three values and `consent-unstated`, and a `declared` slot appears on the install screen.\n- Two gaps remain:\n  - the consumed marker has no stated home or bound (item 2);\n  - another member can spend a ratification on an act its signer did not intend (item 2).\n- Private material would land in the room journal (item 1).\n\nChanges requested.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe note cuts the earlier draft's four acts and per-member presentations down to one value, three acts with no guards, and one rule. Its slices BO1 to BO4 each have one witness. That is the right size. Item 1 may need one thing back on the private side. If it does, give that one thing and nothing more. Approved as to size.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested (item 1).\n- Security: changes requested (items 1 and 2).\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - the catalogue numbers in sections 3 to 5 map to the right cases, except for the \"built\" claim and the two missing cases (item 4);\n  - section 9 against sections 5 to 8 and the contract: items 1, 2, 5 and 7;\n  - the public-material rules: met.\n\nA recut that settles items 1 to 7 needs only the changed text checked again, against these points.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:65f0a1bd55124fd66e75a46036cc80993e5709be","binding":"assigned","head":"8759bbf492061a66013eec1ab3354a6ef1edc984","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:938285671be0ccd765b451e01def6d0d1ca5c1a0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6f798ee7856d573469b23f1e863f393b8b4c678d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:938285671be0ccd765b451e01def6d0d1ca5c1a0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}